WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.679
Film Courage: Going back to the question ‘How
Does the Film and Television Industry Work?’

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:06.300
Let’s suppose someone has a reputable agent.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.080
This writer has checked them out and they
know that they’re in good standing, there’s

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.700
no grievances or complaints.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.170
And then they’ve had this agent submit on
behalf of them to a studio, what’s the process

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.170
like?

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.170
What should they expect?

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.170
What’s the timeframe?

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.840
What if the work is optioned?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.840
What happens?

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.359
Kathie Fong Yoneda: That’s a lot of questions
at one time.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:32.550
Certainly it does take time, there are more
than a 1,000 pieces of material submitted

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.440
every month to the studios.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.110
Maybe slightly less to larger production companies,
but that is a lot of material.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.110
Somebody has got to read it.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.140
It’s got to go up a chain of command so
it does take a little while.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.399
I would say reasonable time, they usually
get things done, read it and will be able

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:55.700
to tell you in 4 to 6 weeks roughly.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.470
That’s what it used to be when I was working
at the studios.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:07.139
It could be slightly longer now, although
a lot of times, now everything is on computer

00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:09.299
so you can just read it at your leisure.

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:13.640
You don’t have to wait for things to be
mailed and that kind of stuff.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:21.729
But I do think that a lot of times it also
depends on who else is submitting material.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:25.909
There may be a more important agent or there
may be an agent that has a script that has

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:29.090
some famous actor or director attached to
it.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.159
They are going to get preference.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:37.630
So sometimes scripts get pushed down a little
lower, a little lower, a little lower so you

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.240
do have to watch out for that.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.399
I do think it’s important that people understand
that for first-timers, you’re going to be

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.139
there up toward the bottom.

00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:53.850
So it probably would be longer, four to six
weeks.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.529
And if you don’t hear anything, what you
do is you just email or call your agent and

00:01:58.529 --> 00:02:01.819
just ask them about it.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.200
Also find out from them if they’ve heard
anything because usually they don't just submit

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.200
it to one place.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.459
They usually try to submit it to a few places
because they want to get a consensus of what

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:17.540
(in general) the producers and the studio
people are looking for.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.540
Film Courage: Well let’s suppose somebody
says “We like it.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.629
It’s a go!”

00:02:21.629 --> 00:02:22.629
What happens?

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:23.629
What are some of the possibilities?

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:27.150
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Well first of all, I don’t
think they are going to say it right away!

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:32.740
[Laughs] But they will probably say “We
are interested in this, can you tell us more?”

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.790
And one of the other questions they are going
to ask…

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.480
“What else do you have?”

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:43.041
Because they want to know (if they like your
writing) what else do you have?

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:44.549
What genre is it?

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:51.900
Is this somebody that…a writer that we want
to get a jump on their other material?

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.250
They are always thinking ahead because they
don’t want to just put all their eggs in

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.250
one basket.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.830
They want to know that there is somebody that’s
got free-flowing talent that’s going to

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.740
continue to build a relationship on.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.799
And that’s why agents are always going to
ask that of you before they take you on as

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:08.799
a client.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:09.799
Film Courage: “What else do you have?”

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:14.690
Kathie Fong Yoneda: “What else to you have?”
that…and because the studio and the producers

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:18.230
always ask “What else does this guy have?”

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.859
they want to know does this writer have more
material because they would like to look at

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:24.059
more material.

